From 6c23d0db78aa1f3a5d1deda20b6635ba03fe60af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Joldersma <bennidhamma@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:25:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] [Linux] Glass frame on Wayland compositors with
 background blur

Enable the cross-platform Glass Frame (GlassFrameService,
FrameStyle::kGlass) on Linux, with the compositor providing the blur
through ext-background-effect-v1 instead of NSGlassEffectView:

- features::IsGlassFrameEnabled() covers Linux; GlassFrameService is
  created there and only makes windows eligible while the platform
  reports background blur support. The Intel-Mac x86 default-off no
  longer applies to Linux. Flag, pref, settings toggle and the
  interactive tests are enabled on Linux.
- BrowserFrameViewLinux paints a translucent frame-colored tint over
  the glass area, reports it via GetGlassFrameRegion(), and keeps it in
  sync with layout, the vertical tab strip and the top container.
  BrowserDesktopWindowTreeHostLinux removes it from the opaque region
  and sends it, clipped to the rounded window shape, as the blur
  region.
- On Linux the whole top chrome is glass: the toolbar, bookmarks bar,
  active tab and tab strip buttons become translucent
  (GlassFrameToolbarOpacity), menus of glass windows are translucent
  (GlassFrameMenuOpacity), and chrome:// pages are see-through with
  semi-opaque cards (GlassFrameTransparentWebUI). An opt-in param makes
  all pages see-through (GlassFrameTransparentWebContents).
- GlassFrameService::GetTintOpacity() is shared with macOS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
